>Remembering one year later: role of the amygdala and the medial temporal lobe memory system in retrieving emotional memories</article-title><source>Proc Natl Acad Sci USA.</source><year>2005</year><volume>102</volume><fpage>2626</fpage><lpage>2631</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">15703295</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib11"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Ellenbogen</surname><given-names>JM</given-names></name><name><surname>Payne</surname><given-names>JD</given-names></name><name><surname>Stickgold</surname><given-names>R</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>The role of sleep in declarative memory consolidation: passive, permissive, active or none?</article-title><source>Curr Opin Neurobiol.</source><year>2006</year><volume>16</volume><fpage>716</fpage><lpage>722</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">17085038</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib12"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Fishbein</surname><given-names>W</given-names></name><name><surname>Kastaniotis</surname><given-names>C</given-names></name><name><surname>Chattman</surname><given-names>D</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Paradoxical sleep: prolonged augmentation following learning</article-title><source>Brain Res.</source><year>1974</year><volume>79</volume><fpage>61</fpage><lpage>75</lpage><pub-id pub-id-type="pmid">4371907</pub-id></citation></ref><ref id="bib13"><citation citation-type="journal"><person-group person-group-type="author"><name><surname>Graves</surname><given-names>LA</given-names></name><name><surname>Heller</surname><given-names>EA</given-names></name><name><surname>Pack</surname><given-names>AI</given-names></name><name><surname>Abel</surname><given-names>T</given-names></name></person-group><article-title>Sleep deprivation selectively impairs memory consolidation for contextual fear conditioning</article-title><source>Learn Mem.</source>